`dpll_satisfiable` sends the CNF of the test expression into `dpll_int_repr` as eight integer clauses. In `dpll_int_repr`, `find_unit_clause_int_repr` finds no unit clause initially, so the first `while P:` does not reduce anything. Then `find_pure_symbol_int_repr` repeatedly finds pure symbols and `unit_propagate_int_repr` removes every clause containing them: this eliminates the clauses involving `F`, then `E`, `D`, `C`, and one of `A`/`B`, leaving only the two clauses corresponding to `(G | H)` and `(~G | ~H)`. Therefore, in the top-level invocation, the loop `for c in clauses:` iterates over exactly 2 clauses.

